C('A linear count','count = 0\nfor _ in range(7):\n    count += 1\nprint(count)','7',['6','8','1'],'One increment occurs for each of the seven iterations.')
C('Independent nested loops','count = 0\nfor i in range(3):\n    for j in range(4):\n        count += 1\nprint(count)','12',['7','3','4'],'Every one of three outer iterations performs four inner increments.')
C('A triangular loop','count = 0\nfor i in range(5):\n    for j in range(i):\n        count += 1\nprint(count)','10',['25','15','5'],'The row lengths are 0, 1, 2, 3 and 4, summing to 10.')
C('Sequential scans','n = 6\ncount = 0\nfor _ in range(n):\n    count += 1\nfor _ in range(n):\n    count += 1\nprint(count)','12',['36','6','7'],'The loops execute sequentially, so their counts add rather than multiply.')
C('Repeated halving','n = 32\ncount = 0\nwhile n > 1:\n    n //= 2\n    count += 1\nprint(count)','5',['32','16','6'],'The successive values are 16, 8, 4, 2 and 1.')
C('Repeated doubling','i = 1\ncount = 0\nwhile i < 20:\n    i *= 2\n    count += 1\nprint(count)','5',['4','20','10'],'The five updates produce 2, 4, 8, 16 and 32, then the condition fails.')
C('Early termination count','checks = 0\nfor x in [4, 8, 12, 16]:\n    checks += 1\n    if x == 8:\n        break\nprint(checks)','2',['1','4','3'],'The match occurs on the second item and break prevents later checks.')
C('Absent target count','checks = 0\nfor x in [4, 8, 12, 16]:\n    checks += 1\n    if x == 9:\n        break\nprint(checks)','4',['0','2','3'],'No item matches, so the loop examines all four entries.')
C('A fixed inner loop','count = 0\nfor i in range(8):\n    for j in range(3):\n        count += 1\nprint(count)','24',['11','64','8'],'The fixed three-step inner loop gives exactly 3 * 8 increments.')
C('A constant calculation','def f(xs):\n    return xs[0] + xs[-1]\nprint(f([2, 9, 4, 6]))','8',['21','2','6'],'Only the first and last entries are added; the middle entries are not scanned.')
C('Summing odd work','n = 4\ncount = 0\nfor i in range(n):\n    count += 2 * i + 1\nprint(count)','16',['10','8','15'],'The odd numbers 1 + 3 + 5 + 7 sum to 16 = n squared.')
C('Skipping alternate items','print(len(range(0, 11, 2)))','6',['5','11','2'],'The range contains 0, 2, 4, 6, 8 and 10.')
C('A shrinking loop','i = 10\nc = 0\nwhile i > 0:\n    i -= 3\n    c += 1\nprint(c)','4',['3','10','7'],'The values after updates are 7, 4, 1 and -2; four iterations are needed.')
C('Products versus sums','n = 4\nprint(n * n, n + n)','16 8',['8 16','16 16','8 8'],'Nested n-by-n work has n squared steps; two separate n-step scans have 2n.')
C('Observed doubling ratio','t_n, t_2n = 0.25, 1.0\nprint(t_2n / t_n)','4.0',['2.0','0.25','0.75'],'The larger observed duration is four times the smaller, consistent with but not proof of quadratic scaling.')
C('Batch normalization','total_seconds = 0.6\ncalls = 3\nprint(f"{total_seconds / calls:.2f}")','0.20',['1.80','0.60','3.00'],'A per-call estimate divides the measured batch duration by the number of calls.')
C('Maximum scan updates','best = -1\nupdates = 0\nfor x in [3, 2, 5, 4]:\n    if x > best:\n        best = x\n        updates += 1\nprint(best, updates)','5 2',['5 4','4 2','5 3'],'All values are checked, but only 3 and 5 exceed the running maximum.')
C('Linear function at two sizes','def work(n):\n    return 3 * n + 2\nprint(work(10), work(20))','32 62',['32 64','30 60','12 22'],'Substitution gives 32 and 62; the constant term means the finite-size ratio is not exactly two.')
C('Pair enumeration','n = 4\npairs = [(i, j) for i in range(n) for j in range(i + 1, n)]\nprint(len(pairs))','6',['16','8','4'],'There are n(n-1)/2 unordered distinct pairs, here 4 * 3 / 2.')
C('Counting comparisons in a scan','xs = [6, 2, 8, 1, 5]\nbest = xs[0]\nc = 0\nfor x in xs[1:]:\n    c += 1\n    if x < best:\n        best = x\nprint(best, c)','1 4',['1 5','2 4','1 1'],'The first value initializes best; the remaining four require one comparison each.')
C('Two growing dimensions','rows, cols = 3, 5\nprint(sum(1 for r in range(rows) for c in range(cols)))','15',['8','25','9'],'The rectangular traversal visits every row-column pair once.')
C('Work after a break','count = 0\nfor i in range(100):\n    count += 1\n    break\nprint(count)','1',['100','0','99'],'The unconditional break stops the loop on its first iteration.')
C('A logarithmic trace','n = 81\nc = 0\nwhile n > 1:\n    n //= 3\n    c += 1\nprint(c)','4',['3','27','81'],'The updates are 27, 9, 3 and 1: four divisions by three.')
C('A dominant term at a finite size','n = 10\nprint(n*n + 3*n + 5)','135',['100','130','115'],'The exact count retains every term even though n squared dominates asymptotically.')
C('Worst of several counts','counts = [1, 4, 7, 10]\nprint(max(counts), sum(counts))','10 22',['22 10','10 10','4 22'],'The maximum describes the largest count in this set; the sum totals all four observed cases.')
T('Under a bounded-scalar model, two sequential n-item scans have tight growth:', 'Theta(n)','Theta(n squared)','Theta(log n)','Theta(1)','The counts add to 2n, and constant factors do not change tight asymptotic growth.')
T('Two fully nested n-iteration loops with constant work per pair have tight growth:', 'Theta(n squared)','Theta(n)','Theta(log n)','Theta(1)','There are n times n iterations of the selected constant-cost operation.')
T('What must be defined before giving a meaningful complexity claim?', 'Input size, input case, operation model, and output contract','Only the function’s name','Only the computer’s brand','Only the source line count','Complexity measures selected work as a function of a stated size and case under assumptions.')
T('Big-O describes:', 'An asymptotic upper bound on growth','An exact runtime in seconds','A guarantee of best possible performance','Only the worst input automatically','Big-O is a bound; the case and cost function being bounded must still be stated.')
T('Which is the tight class of 8n + 30?', 'Theta(n)','Theta(n squared)','Theta(log n)','Theta(1)','For sufficiently large n, this function grows proportionally to n.')
T('Which is the tight class of n squared + 100n?', 'Theta(n squared)','Theta(n)','Theta(n cubed)','Theta(log n)','The quadratic term eventually dominates the lower-order linear term.')
T('An integer loop starts with n > 1 and repeats n //= 2 while n > 1. Its iteration growth is:', 'Theta(log n)','Theta(n)','Theta(n squared)','Theta(1)','Integer halving reaches 1 after a number of iterations proportional to the logarithm of the initial value.')
T('For linear search in a nonempty list, the best case is:', 'A match at the first item','A missing target','A match at the final item','Always n comparisons','A first-position match requires a constant number of examined items.')
T('For ordinary linear search, an absent target requires:', 'Examining every item in the worst case','Examining only the middle item','No comparisons','Sorting is automatically performed','Without ordering or an index, an unchecked item could still contain the target.')
T('Why can an algorithm with better asymptotic growth be slower on small inputs?', 'Constants, overheads and input sizes affect finite runtimes','Asymptotic analysis forbids small inputs','Big-O gives exact seconds','Small programs ignore hardware','Growth predicts trends under a model, while overheads matter at actual finite sizes.')
T('Which doubling ratio is suggested by quadratic work for large n?', 'Approximately 4','Approximately 1','Approximately 2','Approximately 8','Replacing n by 2n multiplies n squared by four under the same dominant-cost model.')
T('Which doubling ratio is suggested by linear work dominated by its leading term?', 'Approximately 2','Approximately 4','Approximately 8','Approximately 0.5','The leading n term doubles; fixed overhead can distort measurements at smaller sizes.')
T('Why repeat timing trials?', 'To reveal variability and support a more stable summary','To force every duration to be identical','To prove the algorithm optimal','To change the expected output','Scheduling, caches and other environmental factors can vary from trial to trial.')
T('Which belongs outside a timing interval when measuring only the computation?', 'Unrelated file loading and display setup','The computation being compared','The required result construction','Every operation that favors one implementation','The timing boundary must match the stated measurement question and apply equally.')
T('What distinguishes an operation-count model from a timing measurement?', 'Counts describe selected work; timings also depend on the execution environment','Counts are always seconds','Timings are independent of hardware','Counts remove the need to state assumptions','Both are useful evidence but answer different questions and have different limitations.')
T('Which sorting of common growth classes is increasing for sufficiently large n?', '1, log n, n, n log n, n squared','n squared, n, log n, 1, n log n','log n, 1, n squared, n, n log n','n, n log n, 1, log n, n squared','This is the standard asymptotic ordering; finite small values need not show every separation.')
T('A fixed 20-step inner loop inside an n-step outer loop gives:', 'Theta(n)','Theta(n squared)','Theta(log n)','Theta(20 to the n)','The factor 20 is constant with respect to n.')
T('A triangular pair loop with n(n-1)/2 iterations has tight growth:', 'Theta(n squared)','Theta(n)','Theta(log n)','Theta(1)','The leading quadratic term remains after dropping constants and lower-order terms.')
T('Why is source line count a poor runtime model?', 'One line may scan, copy, sort, or call substantial work','Every line costs exactly one operation','Short code always runs fastest','Comments dominate runtime','Language constructs can hide work whose cost depends on input size.')
T('Which claim about O(n squared) and Theta(n) is correct?', 'A linear function can also satisfy the looser O(n squared) bound','They always state the same tight growth','O(n squared) proves at least quadratic growth','Theta(n) specifies exact seconds','An upper bound need not be tight; use Theta when stating matching upper and lower growth.')
T('What does an average-case claim require?', 'A stated distribution or probabilistic assumptions about inputs','Only a single convenient example','The maximum measured time','Ignoring input order','Expected work depends on how input cases are weighted.')
T('Which operation is constant-time under the ordinary bounded-value list model?', 'Accessing xs[i] at a valid known index','Copying all of xs','Summing xs','Checking an absent value by list membership','Direct index access does not scan the list; the other operations can visit n entries.')
T('When timing a mutating sort repeatedly, what is necessary?', 'Restore equivalent input conditions for each trial','Reuse already sorted data while calling it random every time','Change the required order between trials','Exclude all comparisons from the interval','Later trials otherwise operate on different input cases and can bias the comparison.')
T('A quadratic model fits three measurements. What can be concluded?', 'The measurements are consistent with that model over the tested range','All larger sizes must follow exactly','Every machine will have the same duration','The algorithm is mathematically proved quadratic','Limited timing observations support a range-limited inference, not a universal proof.')
T('Why state a bounded-numeric classroom model?', 'Arbitrary-precision numeric operations can have value-dependent costs','Python integers have a fixed overflow limit','All integer operations are physically instantaneous','It removes every hidden list cost','Treating scalar arithmetic as constant cost is an explicit simplifying assumption.')
